@twoertwein Getting back to this: This in fact is the expected behavior for the python engine. Header is set to infer -> we think that the first line is the column name and the second line are the names of the index names, because the 2 names plus the column name equal 3, the length of your data rows (it assumes that the mi headers are on the second line).

If you pass a third element to the second line, it shows a warning, also if you only pass element on the second line

Summary: If elements on first and second row together equal the number of elements on the remaining rows, we think column names and multiindex names are given on 2 rows, you should be able to override this with index_col=False, which does not work right now
